KO enters negotiations treating **Finance** and **Foreign Affairs** as senior
claims. Taking either usually requires a harsh renegotiation or a different
balance of power. PSL treats **Agriculture** as a hard coalition claim; Lewica
cannot simply spend leverage through that veto.

Each portfolio controls one Government Affairs card. **Cabinet Reshuffle**
reopens the live allocation, **Coalition Council** manages disputes between
partners, and **Social Welfare** tests collective delivery across ministries.

- @menu: Back

@economy

= Two different currencies

**Political resources** represent party cash, organisers, staff capacity,
volunteers and usable relationships. They are generally uncapped. The alliance
collects its annual income once when January begins; there is no automatic
monthly refund. Annual income combines membership dues with a subvention
calculated from the seats won at the last Sejm election. Raising dues brings an
immediate receipt and a larger January collection, but burdens the party's
least affluent base. Emergency appeals share a long cooldown even when a
different treasurer or adviser makes the call.

**Budget capacity** represents a government's room to fund additional promises.
It is also uncapped and may be negative after an overcommitted settlement.
Opposition parties do not control it. Their annual budget game normally ends in
a vote against the government, a costed alternative, or a deliberately costly
issue deal. A governing coalition sets the next year's capacity through its
internal, coalition, Sejm and Senate budget stages.

- @menu: Back

@polling

= Polls and voters

Eight Polish electorate blocs sum to the country: metropolitan liberals,
liberal professionals, public-service families, industrial and logistics
voters, provincial welfare voters, rural localists, older welfare households
and anti-establishment youth.

Every party competes inside every bloc. Positions, organisation, credibility,
turnout and campaign actions produce vote intention, a sampled headline poll
and a national d'Hondt indicator.

PiS, KO, PSL, Konfederacja and Poland 2050 spend separate organisational pools
in the background. Their pairwise relations stay hidden; the ledger reports
only coalition compatibility and projected seats.

- @menu: Back

@foreign

= Europe, Washington and foreign pressure

The External affairs dossier shares the same three-card agenda as domestic
politics. Drawing a diplomatic opportunity therefore means not drawing an
organising, media or government-delivery card into that slot.

The European campaign builds four distinct assets: a permanent Brussels
mission, a social-policy network, an eastern security coalition and regular
Warsaw–Berlin–Paris work. Completing any three with sufficient influence can
produce a Warsaw compact. It is a political programme, not an automatic treaty
or a generic relations bonus.

Five recurring pressure cards cover EU conditionality, German industrial
demand, the eastern flank, Washington and Europe's radical right. The 2020 and
2024 American elections are seeded and variable. Their result changes the
White House administration, NATO reliability, democratic pressure and later
Ukraine and diplomatic branches. Congress, the ambassador, defence officials
and the Polish Palace retain separate relationship values.

Every pressure card begins with one factual file and then changes its verbs,
costs and consequences with Lewica's authority. Ordinary opposition can ask
questions, move committee business, publish a shadow doctrine, conduct party
diplomacy and build public pressure. Confidence-and-supply can add limited
budget or confidence conditions. A junior coalition party without Foreign
Affairs uses coalition council, pressure, dissent and traded support; with the
portfolio it operates inside the Prime Minister's mandate and can be
overruled. A Lewica-led cabinet directs another party's Foreign Minister, or
acts with full initiative and responsibility when it also holds the
portfolio. Caretakers preserve continuity and defer irreversible commitments.
A Left President represents Poland and uses ratification, appointments and
constitutional influence, never routine cabinet command.

- @menu: Back

@rules

= Prototype rules

Each month:

1. Draw from Party Affairs; from the sixteen-card Government Affairs deck when
   Lewica holds office in a functioning cabinet; from Negotiate with Government
   when Lewica is outside cabinet; or from Foreign Affairs. All four feed one
   persistent three-card agenda.
2. Play one card and choose its political resolution. Playing the card spends
the month; resources return only through choices and the annual income event.
3. Open one of the three active advisor portraits. All advisor interventions
share a six-month cooldown.
4. Use the leadership-bureau card to edit the three-seat slate through the five
political caucuses. A changed slate locks for six months; an unchanged
inspection does not. Or deliberately **End month** without acting and accept
the neglect penalty.
5. Resolve the dated national event.

The separate 2023 route adds election results, coalition arrangements,
confidence arithmetic, ministry allocation and the first coalition budget.
The overview and monthly event desk always name the cabinet, prime minister,
president and Lewica's actual role. Supporting one opposition amendment never
silently turns Lewica into a governing party.

- @menu: Back

@conversion

= Conversion boundary

The active Polish root, router and endings remain isolated from inherited
German events. Selected base-mod patterns have been adapted into Polish
elections, seat charts, coalition arrangements and ministries. Cabinet
Reshuffle, Coalition Council and Social Welfare restore reusable government
patterns with Polish coalition rules and flavour.

